MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

> My question is: what do the errors on the latent variables ("latent1 ~~ x*latent1") mean in this case? ... would it be the errors on the linear models for the latent variables (like "latent1 =~ gamma1_1*endo1")?

Yes, you answered your own question.

> Wouldn't it then make more sense to leave them to be estimated freely, as enforced by ("latent1 ~~ NA*latent1")?

The latent (residual or total) variance is not identified because you haven't measured it (only its indicators).  So you have to "give it a scale" by either fixing its (residual or total) variance to 1 or by fixing a factor loading to 1 (std.lv <http://std.lv> = FALSE). The choice is arbitrary because the two choices lead to statistically equivalent models, and the standardized solutions will be identical (although SEs for model parameters are not proportionally equivalent, in general).

Terrence D. Jorgensen
Postdoctoral Researcher, Methods and Statistics
Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam
UvA web page: http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen

